WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:02.360
How do memoisation and tabulation

2
00:00:02.360 --> 00:00:04.880
remove repeated work? We will

3
00:00:04.880 --> 00:00:07.880
compute Fibonacci six, distinguish repeated

4
00:00:07.880 --> 00:00:10.020
calls from distinct states, and

5
00:00:10.020 --> 00:00:11.880
derive both a recursive cached

6
00:00:11.880 --> 00:00:13.880
solution and a bottom-up table.

7
00:00:14.380 --> 00:00:16.960
The ordinary recursive function asks

8
00:00:16.960 --> 00:00:19.260
for Fibonacci five and Fibonacci

9
00:00:19.260 --> 00:00:21.120
four to compute Fibonacci six.

10
00:00:21.620 --> 00:00:23.680
Fibonacci five then asks for

11
00:00:23.680 --> 00:00:25.800
Fibonacci four again, so two

12
00:00:25.800 --> 00:00:27.940
branches immediately repeat the same

13
00:00:27.940 --> 00:00:28.960
mathematical question.

14
00:00:29.380 --> 00:00:31.320
Continuing the call tree repeats

15
00:00:31.320 --> 00:00:33.360
smaller inputs many more times.

16
00:00:34.060 --> 00:00:36.440
The uncached recursion has exponential

17
00:00:36.440 --> 00:00:38.500
growth, with big O of

18
00:00:38.500 --> 00:00:39.740
two to the n as

19
00:00:39.740 --> 00:00:41.600
a valid upper bound, even

20
00:00:41.600 --> 00:00:43.200
though its maximum call depth

21
00:00:43.200 --> 00:00:44.260
is only linear.

22
00:00:44.860 --> 00:00:47.520
Dynamic programming identifies a state

23
00:00:47.520 --> 00:00:49.200
by the information needed to

24
00:00:49.200 --> 00:00:51.380
determine its answer. Here that

25
00:00:51.380 --> 00:00:53.500
information is just the integer

26
00:00:53.500 --> 00:00:56.060
index, so all calls requesting

27
00:00:56.060 --> 00:00:58.000
Fibonacci four can share one

28
00:00:58.000 --> 00:00:58.800
stored result.

29
00:00:59.300 --> 00:01:02.080
In top-down memoisation, check the

30
00:01:02.080 --> 00:01:03.520
cache before solving a state

31
00:01:03.520 --> 00:01:05.200
again. If an answer is

32
00:01:05.200 --> 00:01:07.600
present, return it; otherwise obtain

33
00:01:07.600 --> 00:01:09.500
the smaller answers, add them,

34
00:01:09.820 --> 00:01:11.280
store the result and return

35
00:01:11.280 --> 00:01:11.580
it.

36
00:01:11.900 --> 00:01:13.940
Zero is a valid Fibonacci

37
00:01:13.940 --> 00:01:16.080
answer, so it cannot double

38
00:01:16.080 --> 00:01:17.960
as the unknown marker. An

39
00:01:17.960 --> 00:01:20.060
initially unused sentinel or a

40
00:01:20.060 --> 00:01:22.260
separate presence flag distinguishes an

41
00:01:22.260 --> 00:01:23.840
unsolved state from the already

42
00:01:23.840 --> 00:01:25.160
computed value zero.

43
00:01:25.780 --> 00:01:27.900
Collapse the repeated call tree

44
00:01:27.900 --> 00:01:29.500
into a graph with one

45
00:01:29.500 --> 00:01:31.640
vertex for each index from

46
00:01:31.640 --> 00:01:33.800
zero to six. An arrow

47
00:01:33.800 --> 00:01:35.140
here points from a caller

48
00:01:35.140 --> 00:01:36.320
to a state it depends

49
00:01:36.320 --> 00:01:38.340
on, so the destination must

50
00:01:38.340 --> 00:01:39.860
be solved before that caller

51
00:01:39.860 --> 00:01:40.400
can finish.

52
00:01:41.020 --> 00:01:43.820
Bottom-up tabulation uses those dependencies

53
00:01:43.820 --> 00:01:45.780
to select an evaluation order.

54
00:01:46.140 --> 00:01:47.220
Set the first two cells

55
00:01:47.220 --> 00:01:48.760
to zero and one, then

56
00:01:48.760 --> 00:01:50.580
visit indices from two onward

57
00:01:50.580 --> 00:01:52.420
so both preceding answers already

58
00:01:52.420 --> 00:01:52.880
exist.

59
00:01:53.620 --> 00:01:55.700
At index two, add zero

60
00:01:55.700 --> 00:01:56.920
and one to get one;

61
00:01:57.280 --> 00:01:59.100
at index three, add one

62
00:01:59.100 --> 00:02:00.120
and one to get two.

63
00:02:00.660 --> 00:02:02.500
At index four, the two

64
00:02:02.500 --> 00:02:04.280
preceding answers are one and

65
00:02:04.280 --> 00:02:05.560
two, so the result is

66
00:02:05.560 --> 00:02:05.920
three.

67
00:02:06.700 --> 00:02:08.600
At index five the sum

68
00:02:08.600 --> 00:02:10.580
is two plus three, giving

69
00:02:10.580 --> 00:02:12.680
five. At index six the

70
00:02:12.680 --> 00:02:14.100
sum is three plus five,

71
00:02:14.280 --> 00:02:15.820
giving eight, so the final

72
00:02:15.820 --> 00:02:18.900
table is zero, one, one,

73
00:02:19.160 --> 00:02:22.320
two, three, five, eight.

74
00:02:22.980 --> 00:02:24.400
There are n plus one

75
00:02:24.400 --> 00:02:26.460
possible states and constant arithmetic

76
00:02:26.460 --> 00:02:28.380
work for each evaluated state,

77
00:02:28.780 --> 00:02:31.180
giving linear arithmetic time. A

78
00:02:31.180 --> 00:02:32.860
full table uses linear space,

79
00:02:33.360 --> 00:02:35.160
and the top-down version also

80
00:02:35.160 --> 00:02:37.260
has a linear-depth recursion stack.

81
00:02:37.900 --> 00:02:39.680
If only the final Fibonacci

82
00:02:39.680 --> 00:02:42.300
value is required, bottom-up evaluation

83
00:02:42.300 --> 00:02:44.080
needs just the two previous

84
00:02:44.080 --> 00:02:46.420
values. That reduces extra storage

85
00:02:46.420 --> 00:02:47.680
to a constant number of

86
00:02:47.680 --> 00:02:49.020
machine words under the course

87
00:02:49.020 --> 00:02:52.100
arithmetic model; exact large-integer bit

88
00:02:52.100 --> 00:02:53.660
costs are a separate analysis.

89
00:02:54.260 --> 00:02:55.920
Things to take note of:

90
00:02:56.100 --> 00:02:58.260
define the state, distinguish calls

91
00:02:58.260 --> 00:03:00.480
from first computations, and respect

92
00:03:00.480 --> 00:03:03.660
the dependency direction. Memoisation is

93
00:03:03.660 --> 00:03:05.760
still recursive and DP is

94
00:03:05.760 --> 00:03:08.400
not automatically polynomial; its cost

95
00:03:08.400 --> 00:03:09.780
follows the number of states

96
00:03:09.780 --> 00:03:11.980
and transitions, not the comforting

97
00:03:11.980 --> 00:03:13.460
sound of the word cache.
